            <ArticleTitle>Identifying patterns of obesity risk behavior to improve pediatric primary care.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>18-28</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/jspn.12131</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To develop profiles of obesity risk behaviors for children and adolescents.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Risk assessments were obtained from patients (n = 971) at a school-based health center. Latent class analysis was used to create subgroups based on seven indicators measuring diet, activity, and screen time.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Four classes emerged, with 44% classified as the &quot;Healthiest,&quot; 8% as the &quot;Least Healthy,&quot; 37% as &quot;Mixed Diet/Low Activity/Low Screen Time,&quot; and 11% as &quot;Mixed Diet/High Activity/High Screen Time.&quot; Several demographic predictors distinguished the classes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRACTICE IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Obesity risk factor profiles may help providers identify strengths and risks, tailor counseling, and plan interventions with families.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015, Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
